84:- [[REDACTED]02T14:58:36Z] quant-skeptic/verification — ❌ REFUTED VERIFY: ad-hoc claim: {"finding_topic": "ad-hoc claim", "verdict": "REFUTED", "confidence": "high", "checks": {"look_ahead_leak": "na — retroactive bookkeeping/tax correction on historical fills, not a live trading filter; no forward column touched", "oos_robustness": "na — not a backtest, no walk-forward applicable", "panel_curation_bias": "na — not a universe/panel selection", "reproducibility_selfcheck": "pass — `python3 mike/bin/dividend_adjusted_return.py --selfcheck` reproduces 58/58 PASS live, and identically under `env -u TZ` and `TZ=America/New_York LC_ALL=C`; raw `dnse_raw_[REDACTED]/17.jsonl` (account-filtered) match the report's cited numbers to the VND, and `nav_snapshot_[REDACTED]_[REDACTED].json`'s independent `offbook_assets`=302,108,211 corroborates the withdrawal figure from a second source", "param_overfit": "na — 5% is a statutory withholding rate (Luật 109/2025/QH15, verified via live web search: passed 2025-12-10, effective [REDACTED], capital-investment income stays 5% for resident individuals), not a tuned parameter", "capacity_adv_realism": "na — not a capacity/liquidity question", "arithmetic_mechanism": "fail — the report's own status line ('Trạng thái: ... 3 báo cáo tháng 7 đã phát hành lại (bản sửa #2)') is false. `git status` in the mike/ repo shows `bin/dividend_adjusted_return.py` modified-but-UNCOMMITTED and the research report file UNTRACKED. `git log` on the 3 target report files shows only commit 516e3c43 (bản sửa #1, the gross dividend-inclusion fix) — no second commit exists. A repo-wide grep for the tax-adjusted NET figures (608,750 / 11,566,250 / 6,130,825 / the NET percentages) finds zero hits anywhere under mike/reports/. File mtimes confirm the 3 reports (13:55-13:57 ICT) predate the code/report artifacts for this job (21:44-21:49 ICT) by ~8 hours — the reissue could not have happened yet."}, "independent_recompute": "Re-ran `--selfcheck` live (58/58 PASS, reproduced under 2 TZ variants); independently recomputed the totalCash identity and the 5.00% tax rate directly from the raw dnse_raw_[REDACTED]/17.jsonl records (account-filtered) — matched the report's numbers exactly to the VND; independently verified via WebSearch that Luật 109/2025/QH15 (passed 2025-12-10, effective [REDACTED]) keeps the 5% rate on capital-investment income for resident individuals.", "killer_objection": "The finding's own status line claims the 3 client-facing reports have already been reissued with the tax-adjusted (net) numbers — this is false. git shows the tax code is uncommitted, the research report is untracked, and none of the 3 report files on disk contain any tax-adjusted figure. Right now, investors reading those reports are still seeing gross (overstated) post-dividend returns, contradicted by a report that says the problem is already fixed.", "recommended_reruns": ["Actually reissue the 3 reports with a 'bản sửa #2' banner + the tax-adjusted NET per-ticker/portfolio numbers, commit both the report edits and mike/bin/dividend_adjusted_return.py, then re-verify the status line matches git reality", "Wait for the second real MBB/BID/CTG/VCB/NCT/SAB settlement event (per the report's own n=1 caveat) and re-run the totalCash-identity measurement to confirm 5.00% again before treating the rate as closed", "Add a cheap guard (e.g. grep-for-claimed-artifact in a pre-report-close check) so a 'đã phát hành lại' status claim can't be asserted without a matching commit touching mike/reports/"], "summary": "The underlying tax finding is real and well-supported: dnse_raw JSONL data (independently re-pulled and account-filtered), the NAV snapshot file, and a live web search against Vietnam's actual Luật 109/2025/QH15 all confirm cashDividendReceiving is gross and a genuine 5% PIT withholding applies at real settlement, measured to the VND on the one real MBB payout event. The code selfcheck reproduces cleanly across environments, and no other consumer of the module is affected. However, the report falsely claims the 3 monthly/weekly client reports have already been reissued with the tax-corrected net numbers — git history, git status, and a repo-wide grep all show this reissue never happened (only the earlier, unrelated gross-dividend-inclusion fix was committed). Because a live trading fleet could reasonably read this finding as 'job done' and stop tracking it while investors continue to see inflated after-tax returns, the finding is REFUTED on its completion claim even though its financial/legal substance is sound — the fix is to actually publish bản sửa #2, not to discard the tax analysis."}
1182:- [[REDACTED]1:37:47Z] quant-skeptic/verification — ❌ REFUTED VERIFY: ad-hoc claim: {"finding_topic": "ad-hoc claim", "verdict": "REFUTED", "confidence": "high", "checks": {"look_ahead_leak": "pass — band_52w() splices monthly points only where mo.index < real.index[0] (strictly before the first daily print), rubber_weekly.py band_52w(); no forward columns involved, this is a monitoring feed not a BQ filter.", "oos_robustness": "na — operational alert gate, not a backtested trading signal.", "panel_curation_bias": "na — single commodity feed, no ticker universe.", "reproducibility_selfcheck": "pass on what it tests, but incomplete — reran rubber_weekly_selfcheck.py myself: 50/50 PASS under env -u TZ, TZ=UTC, TZ=America/New_York, on both system python3/pandas 2.3.3 and wc_venv/pandas 3.0.2 (6 runs), production rubber_alert_state.json verified byte-unchanged after. Independently recomputed the compression-direction claim from raw data/rubber_weekly.csv grouped by month: means 2.693-2.809 vs true daily 2.596-2.919, exact match. However the suite has a real coverage gap: it never tests a multi-day [REDACTED]alert that later reverts (only tests unconfirmed-then-revert [6b] and single-day-confirm-then-fire [6]) — that gap is exactly where I found a real bug (see arithmetic_mechanism).", "param_overfit": "pass with caveat — BAND_MIN_SPAN=330/BAND_MAX_GAP=45 unchanged from round 1, still single-incident judgment with 24d/14d slack. New minor issue: I simulated forward and the band actually hardens (mo goes empty, band_soft flips False) on 2027-06-16, not '~2027-05' as the round-2 writeup claims — about a 5-6 week understatement of how long the softened window persists (~10.3 months from today, not '~9 months'). Cosmetic, doesn't change the design tradeoff.", "capacity_adv_realism": "na — alert threshold, not a trade-size question.", "arithmetic_mechanism": "fail — two real problems in the confirm_alert() fix shipped for Gap 2. (1) Traced bus('status',...) -> append_event.sh -> bus/inbox/Winston.jsonl -> consolidate.sh -> mike_json.py:cmd_delta_append (line 226), which forwards only event_type in ('finding','answer','decision','verification') into kb/recent_delta.jsonl; publish_context.sh's own header states that file's RECENT block is exactly what the UserPromptSubmit hook auto-injects into every session. 'status' events are excluded, so the self-cancel notice is NOT automatically visible to Taylor -- it only reaches the full kb/events_buffer.md log and the raw inbox file. The 'this closes the loop audibly' / 'Taylor already receives... only Telegram/Bill wait' framing overstates actual visibility for the resolution notice specifically (the day-1 unconfirmed ALERT, a 'finding' event, IS correctly auto-visible). (2) Reproduced directly: simulated a 3-day persisting ALERT that gets [REDACTED](Telegram/Bill fire on day 2) then reverts on day 4 -- confirm_alert() still emits 'rubber: ALERT chờ xác nhận đã tự huỷ ... không gửi Telegram/Bill theo thiết kế', which is FALSE (Telegram/Bill were already sent two days earlier). Root cause: pending_alert is set once on first sighting and never cleared/updated after confirmation, so the drop-message code can't distinguish 'never escalated' from 'already escalated, now reverting.' This is a genuine, reproducible defect the 50/50 selfcheck does not cover."}, "independent_recompute": "Ran rubber_weekly_selfcheck.py directly: 50/50 PASS across 3 TZ settings x 2 python/pandas environments, matching the finding's claim exactly; production state file confirmed untouched. Independently hand-recomputed the monthly-average compression direction from raw data/rubber_weekly.csv (means 2.693-2.809 vs true daily 2.596-2.919) -- matches to the digit. Went beyond the selfcheck's own scenarios: simulated forward-dated band hardening (found actual transition date 2027-06-16 vs claimed '~2027-05') and a confirmed-then-reverted multi-day ALERT (found the false 'Telegram/Bill not sent' message described above) -- both via direct calls into the real rubber_weekly.py module, not synthetic dict tests.", "killer_objection": "confirm_alert()'s self-cancel bus message asserts 'Telegram/Bill were not sent, by design' unconditionally whenever a pending/prior ALERT streak ends on a non-ALERT tier -- but I reproduced a realistic scenario (ALERT persists 3 days, gets confirmed, Telegram/Bill fire on day 2, then reverts on day 4) where that message is factually false: the escalation already happened. Combined with the fact that the message's event_type ('status') is excluded from the fleet's actual auto-injected context (kb/recent_delta.jsonl / context_pack.md RECENT block, traced through mike_json.py and publish_context.sh), the round-2 claim that Gap 2 now 'closes the loop audibly' does not hold: the notice is both less visible than claimed and, in a realistic multi-day scenario, wrong.", "recommended_reruns": ["Fix confirm_alert(): clear/update pending_alert (or track a separate 'was_confirmed' flag) once an ALERT is confirmed, so the drop-message correctly distinguishes 'never escalated' from 'already escalated on day N, now reverting' -- and don't claim 'Telegram/Bill not sent' when they were.", "Add a selfcheck case for a multi-day [REDACTED]ALERT that later reverts, asserting the bus message content is accurate in that case (this is the exact gap that let the bug above ship with 50/50 green).", "Either change the self-cancel notice's event_type to 'finding' (or another type mike_json.py's delta-append forwards) so it actually reaches Taylor's auto-injected context, or explicitly document that it's forensics-only and add a human-facing channel for the V-crash-suppression case.", "Correct the '~2027-05' hardening date in the code comment/dispatch note to the simulated actual date (~2027-06-16)."], "summary": "The core production fix -- replacing the row-count 52-week-band gate with a calendar-coverage test, splicing WB monthly data with a correctly-derived soft/hard ALERT distinction -- is solid, well-tested, and I independently reproduced both the 50/50 selfcheck (across 3 timezones and 2 pandas versions) and its headline numbers by hand. Gap 1 from round 1 is genuinely closed. But the round-2 fix for Gap 2 (the ALERT confirmation gate's silent-drop problem) does not hold up under attack: tracing the actual bus-delivery pipeline shows the self-cancel notice's event_type ('status') is excluded from the fleet's auto-injected session context, so the claim that this 'closes the loop audibly' for Taylor is overstated; and testing one step beyond the selfcheck's own scenarios (a multi-day [REDACTED]alert that later reverts) reproduces a genuine bug where the self-cancel message falsely claims Telegram/Bill were never sent, when they already had been. Recommend fixing confirm_alert()'s pending_alert lifecycle and the event-type/visibility issue, adding a regression test for the confirmed-then-reverted case, and re-submitting for a round-3 check before treating this as production-hardened."}
